    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Let me verify the complete picture: there is exactly one concrete test class (`AcceptanceTest`; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract and `AuditLogCapture` is a helper), and it passes.\n\nHere's what I found and confirmed:\n\n## Result\n\nThe specification is fully satisfied and all tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n**The requirement is met by `ExceptionControllerAdvice`** (`src/main/java/.../rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`). Every rejection path returns an RFC7807 body:\n\n- **400 Bad Request** \u2014 `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (Bean Validation, e.g. missing `city`), `InvalidPostcodeException`, `FutureRegistrationDateException`, `DisposableEmailDomainException`\n- **409 Conflict** \u2014 `DuplicateIdentityException`, `CityOwnerLimitException`\n- **429 Too Many Requests** \u2014 `DailyOwnerLimitException`\n\nEach handler builds a Spring `ProblemDetail` via `detailBuild(...)`, which populates all four required members \u2014 `type` (set to the request URL), `title` (exception class simple name), `status` (from `ProblemDetail.forStatus`), and `detail` (a message). Because the handlers return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, Spring MVC automatically serializes them with the `application/problem+json` content type, and the HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\nI verified there are **no other 400/409/429 sources** in the codebase \u2014 grepping `src/main` for `BAD_REQUEST`, `CONFLICT`, `TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`, `ResponseStatusException`, and direct `status(4xx)` returns turned up only these advice-routed exceptions (the idempotency store never returns a 409). So no rejection path bypasses the problem+json format.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` \u2014 which posts an owner missing `city` and asserts `400` + `content-type application/problem+json` + `$.status == 400` \u2014 passes, exercising the validation (400) rejection path end-to-end.",
